WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.660
The next mesh optimization I want to talk about

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.559
is when and where to use Nanite.

3
00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:09.099
Now, Nanite is Unreal Engine 5's new virtualized geometry

4
00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:10.980
system, and it allows you to have

5
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.099
meshes that have triangle counts that

6
00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:15.099
are in the millions.

7
00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:17.780
In the screen in front of us, we see a static mesh

8
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.899
at the top and the same static mesh,

9
00:00:19.899 --> 00:00:23.100
but this time utilizing the Nanite technology.

10
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.140
And if we take a look at the highlighted area,

11
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.859
we can really start to see where Nanite outperforms

12
00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:30.460
traditional static meshes.

13
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.820
This area highlights a ridge and this wooden sign,

14
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.460
and the static mesh, the top, we see some polygonal geometry.

15
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.179
And this is typical of static meshes of this size.

16
00:00:42.179 --> 00:00:43.259
This isn't a huge prop.

17
00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:44.859
This is just a regular sign.

18
00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:48.219
However, with the Nanite mesh, notice in that same area,

19
00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:50.200
we don't really actually see any

20
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.659
of this typical polygonal geometry.

21
00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:55.100
We actually see a fantastic silhouette

22
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.859
that even has dips and peaks in it,

23
00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:00.140
much like we would expect if we were looking at an object

24
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.740
in the real world.

25
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.299
One question you also might be asking yourself is,

26
00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:05.780
when should I use Nanite?

27
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.019
What if I am not using a static mesh

28
00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:10.640
that has millions of triangles?

29
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.739
The answer to that question is whenever possible.

30
00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:16.900
Nanite should be enabled on any and all static meshes

31
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.879
that you're utilizing.

32
00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:19.739
The only reason you shouldn't be using Nanite

33
00:01:19.739 --> 00:01:22.900
is if the platform you're currently developing for

34
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:27.459
doesn't support Nanite, or the object or effect

35
00:01:27.459 --> 00:01:32.340
or item you're working on requires the use of something

36
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.859
that Nanite doesn't support,

37
00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:36.659
such as skeletal mesh animations.

38
00:01:36.659 --> 00:01:40.659
Now, you can enable Nanite on any static mesh

39
00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:43.159
throughout a number of different locations

40
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:45.239
inside of Unreal Engine 5.

41
00:01:45.239 --> 00:01:47.739
The first way to enable Nanite is on import.

42
00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:51.739
When you import your static geometry on import,

43
00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:53.219
there is an option to actually build

44
00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:56.930
the corresponding Nanite mesh for that asset.

45
00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.609
You can also enable and then set settings

46
00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:02.969
for your Nanite meshes inside of the details panel

47
00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:04.810
in your static mesh editor.

48
00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:06.709
You can also use geometry collections

49
00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:09.870
to enable or disable Nanite meshes.

50
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.909
And finally, you can enable and disable Nanite meshes

51
00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:15.050
through the content browser

52
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.449
by selecting the required assets, right-clicking,

53
00:02:18.449 --> 00:02:19.969
and then selecting Nanite,

54
00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:22.740
and then the required Nanite option.

55
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.060
Well, Nanite is an amazing tool

56
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.539
that supports things like static meshes,

57
00:02:27.539 --> 00:02:30.740
instance static meshes, hierarchical static meshes,

58
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.780
hierarchical instanced static meshes,

59
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.219
geometry collection, role position offset,

60
00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:38.020
and spline mesh support.

61
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.659
It unfortunately doesn't support everything.

62
00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:43.460
Things that are not currently supported

63
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.060
include skeletal animations, morph targets,

64
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.740
vertex painting on instances of Nanite meshes,

65
00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.379
deferred decals, and custom UVs.

66
00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:57.819
Now, with that said, the features that Nanite support

67
00:02:57.819 --> 00:02:59.199
are always changing.

68
00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:02.800
In fact, since its release back in Unreal Engine 5.0,

69
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.500
Nanite has actually added support

70
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.240
for both world position offset and spline mesh support.

71
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.939
So be on the lookout for new things

72
00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:13.280
that Nanite can do in future releases of Unreal Engine 5.
